; HelloWorld: the smallest complete proof in the Eunoia proof format.
;
; Two assumptions that contradict each other, and two steps that derive `false`
; from them. It exercises the whole pipeline -- the s-expression reader, the
; parser's operator table, the checker's command loop and its refutation test --
; without needing anything proven about any rule.
;
; Expected verdict: incomplete (see hello.expected).
;
; The checker accepts it as a derivation of `false`, so it is not `incorrect`.
; It is not `correct` either, because that would assert the assumptions are
; unsatisfiable, and the soundness theorem does not apply until the side
; conditions in Proofs/Assumptions.lean say the semantics models these terms.
; A fresh checker reports exactly that, and the verdict improves to `correct`
; when the semantics and the rule proofs are written -- so this test is a
; live readout of how far the development has got.

(declare-const x Int)
(declare-const y Int)
(assume @p0 (= y x))
(assume @p1 (not (= x y)))
(step @p2 :rule symm :premises (@p1))
(step @p3 :rule contra :premises (@p0 @p2))
